WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:03.086
The US Centers for Disease 
Control and Prevention,

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:04.838
otherwise known as the CDC,

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:08.925
has faced some major criticisms during the pandemic,

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:10.844
ultimately highlighting that 
the public health agency

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:14.097
failed to meet the 
world's expectations.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:16.850
So in an effort to learn 
from past mistakes,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.477
its director has 
initiated an overhaul

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:22.772
meant to revamp everything
from the agency's operations

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:24.149
to its culture.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:26.359
I flew down to its 
headquarters in Atlanta

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.070
to ask CDC Director 
Rochelle Walensky

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.572
how the revamp is going.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.705
When you first announced Moving Forward,

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:39.706
you laid out your vision

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:40.874
for the agency and how

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:42.292
it must evolve and here

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:43.460
you said that the agency was,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.465
“responsible for some pretty 
dramatic, pretty public mistakes.”

00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:50.341
Now that was quite candid.

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:52.343
And I wonder what examples

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.137
you were thinking of when 
you shared that message

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:56.139
internally with CDC staff.

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:58.475
It wasn't hard to find 
headlines of places

00:00:58.475 --> 00:00:59.726
where CDC was taking heat.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.187
One of them, of course, 
was the lab

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:05.023
 and the early diagnostic 
test that came out.

00:01:05.023 --> 00:01:06.399
And others have just been 

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.526
confusing and 
complicated messages.

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:09.694
Great news this week.

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:13.323
The CDC announced that fully
vaccinated people no longer have

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:15.867
to wear masks 
or socially distance,
except if you go to most places.

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:19.579
except if you go to most places.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:22.207
Early on, our guidance 
was very technical.

00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:24.542
We were not necessarily 
accustomed to having

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:26.002
 our guidance be a place

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:28.338
where every American 
would click on CDC

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:32.467
and read our school guidance
or read our quarantine guidance.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:34.427
But that's how it was 
being utilized now.

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:35.720
So we had to rise 
to the occasion.

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:39.891
So one of those components
was really delivering our science

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.726
in real time when we knew it.

00:01:41.726 --> 00:01:42.894
And then the 2nd is

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.437
saying it in plain language.

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:45.563
What does this mean?

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:47.190
What are the things 
that it doesn't say?

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.483
What are the things 
that it does say?

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:49.567
And what is still unknown?

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.779
Among the changes, 
Dr. Walensky will require

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.656
all employees be ready to deploy

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:56.324
to combat health crises,

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.035
marking a drastic shift
from a volunteer system

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:02.163
the agency relied on to
staff its Covid-19 response.

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:07.001
It is difficult with 
multiple responses,

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:09.712
to have the same people respond 
again and again and again.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:11.589
And so what we need to do

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:14.008
is to make sure that every 
person in this agency actually

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:18.346
considers response as a 
critical element of their job.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:21.141
There's a number of activities
that we're doing now

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.059
to sort of move in that direction.

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:25.895
I think there's a lot of challenges
that we're overcoming.

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:28.189
We were very siloed 
in the way we respond

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:30.567
to various threats
before Covid-19.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:34.654
The level of the response 
needed really required us to

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:38.241
work across the 
agency to pull together.

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:40.535
What I wanted that
restructuring to reflect

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:43.913
is high visibility, accountability
within the agency and

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:46.291
what are those key places

00:02:46.291 --> 00:02:48.459
that we really need 
to be responsible.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:52.589
So things like our laboratory,
our Office of Science,

00:02:52.589 --> 00:02:54.924
our public health 
infrastructure and workforce.

00:02:54.924 --> 00:02:56.551
I really want to decrease

00:02:56.551 --> 00:02:58.344
layers of reporting,

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.055
decrease the 
bureaucracy, if you will.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:09.230
We're in a BSL-4 
laboratory training facility.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.565
Biosafety level 4.

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:14.402
Yes. We train people
so that they can work safely

00:03:14.402 --> 00:03:16.654
and effectively when 
they're really dealing with

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:18.489
the most pathogenic agents

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:21.492
that we have, things 
like smallpox, Ebola.

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:25.747
When we looked at what's 
happened in the pandemic,

00:03:25.747 --> 00:03:28.958
there were really 3 things
we want to do better.

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:32.295
The 1st is we can't have 
a test that goes out

00:03:32.295 --> 00:03:35.173
that doesn't have a thorough 
enough quality review.

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:37.717
The 2nd thing, we need to get it

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.637
as fast as possible 
to the private sector.

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:42.764
And the 3rd thing, 
we want to make sure

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:44.807
we stay on top of 
the best technologies,

00:03:44.807 --> 00:03:47.560
because we would 
like to have tests

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.604
like the tests that you 
get in drugstores

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:52.148
but to be as accurate 
as the PCR test.

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:54.025
I heard a lot of optimism

00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:56.694
from CDC staff about their 
plans to move forward.

00:03:56.694 --> 00:04:00.198
But one potential 
obstacle is Congress.

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:03.326
Funding is a place we've seen Congress reach a roadblock.

00:04:03.326 --> 00:04:05.453
What kind of resources 
will it take to execute

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:07.997
the strategy that you've laid out?

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:10.959
One of the challenges of CDC,
when I got here was that

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:13.962
we were working off a frail,

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:17.715
underfunded public health 
infrastructure, not just the CDC,

00:04:17.715 --> 00:04:18.633
 but around the country.

00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:20.593
There have been data that 
have suggested that the workforce

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:23.096
the workforce is 80,000

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.847
public health workers in deficit.

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:25.515
Wow.

00:04:25.515 --> 00:04:27.517
When you start with that 
infrastructure and you

00:04:27.517 --> 00:04:29.394
 layer on top of it 
a global pandemic
that affects 330 million Americans,

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:32.397
that affects 
330 million Americans,

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:34.649
you have challenges 
at the get go.

00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:36.401
Moving Forward, in my mind

00:04:36.401 --> 00:04:37.568
is really a lot about

00:04:37.568 --> 00:04:40.154
what's the best, 
most efficient way

00:04:40.154 --> 00:04:42.949
that we can take the resources
that we have right now and

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:46.828
leverage them to do the best 
that we can within CDC.

00:04:46.828 --> 00:04:50.206
There will be areas and 
places where we will ask

00:04:50.206 --> 00:04:53.710
for additional resources in our 
public health infrastructure,

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:58.423
in our data modernization efforts,
in our laboratory capacity.

00:04:58.423 --> 00:05:01.092
And then there will be places
that we fully recognize

00:05:01.092 --> 00:05:02.260
 that we're going to need
congressional help

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.136
in order to be nimble.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.476
I want to talk about 
politics for a moment

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.187
because science 
has become political.

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:16.232
I wonder what the toll of 
that has been on the CDC.

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:19.527
Sometimes it's harder in a 
politically charged environment,

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:23.156
but our job is to deliver 
health in a bipartisan way.

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:26.743
I always go back to when 
I'm standing in front of a patient,

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:29.746
they don't necessarily challenge
the reason that you're there.

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:31.873
They know that you're 
there to care for them.

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:34.625
This agency perceives
the country as their patient.

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:38.087
So the most important thing 
that we do is put our head down, 

00:05:38.087 --> 00:05:40.048
do the science, public health,

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:43.051
action-oriented work 
to deliver health.

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:45.136
We're going to need 
to demonstrate that

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:48.056
regardless of the political 
powers that are out there.

